<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Western Pomerania, Swedish Pomerania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomerania is a historical region on the southern Baltic coast, today divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied the Oder estuary in the 1630s and gained Western Pomerania in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648. Swedish Pomerania gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> a foothold in Germany, though most of it was later lost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomerania.jpg" alt="Pomerania" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calle_Dlugie_Pobrzeze,_Gdansk,_Polonia,_2013-05-20,_DD_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pomerania lies along the Bay of Pomerania on the southern Baltic shore, between the rivers Recknitz, Trebel, Tollense and Augraben in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in the east. Its name is Slavic and means land by the sea. Most of it is coastal lowland on the Central European Plain, with a southern belt of hills and moraine-dammed lakes, the Pomeranian Lake District, and generally poor, sandy or marshy soil. The western coast is jagged, with the islands of Rügen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolin">Wolin</a> and the Lagoon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>.</p><p>The term has no universally accepted definition. German usage means Hither and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Farther Pomerania</a> only, Polish usage adds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, the eastern part, which includes Kashubia, Kociewie, the Tuchola Forest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>. It was first mentioned in an imperial document of 1046, naming Zemuzil, Duke of the Pomeranians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived in the area since the end of the Vistula Glacial Stage some 13,000 years ago; later peoples included Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> and, in the Dark Ages, West Slavic tribes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>. From the 10th century early Polish rulers subdued the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> was established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>. In the late 12th century the western part fell under the suzerainty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and the Duchy of Pomerania became Christian under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a>. In the early 14th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>; it returned to the Polish Crown in 1466 after the Thirteen Years' War. The Duchy of Pomerania adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1534.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> ravaged and depopulated the duchy. When the Griffin house died out, the duchy was divided in 1648 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. Prussia gained southern Swedish Pomerania in 1720, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in 1772 and 1793, and received the rest of Swedish Pomerania in 1815. Both Pomeranian provinces joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. After the First World War Pomerelia returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> became the Free City of Danzig.</p><p>The first battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> was fought at Westerplatte in September 1939. Under German occupation more than 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> died in executions, camps, prisons and forced labour, and the Stutthof concentration camp stood in the region. After 1945 the border moved west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> and the German inhabitants were expelled; the area was resettled mainly by Poles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pomerania is split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its largest cities are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>. The western part, Hither Pomerania, belongs to the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>; most of the rest lies in the Polish West Pomeranian, Pomeranian and Kuyavian-Pomeranian voivodeships. Kashubians, descendants of the medieval West Slavic Pomeranians, are numerous in rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Griffin prince, Eric of Pomerania, became king of Denmark, Norway and Sweden in 1397, and later ended his days as a minor duke in <b>Pomerania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The members remained subject to their own lords, from the emperor to the Teutonic Order and the dukes of <b>Pomerania</b>, which limited the league’s capacity to act as a state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick William, the Great Elector (r. 1640–1688), won full sovereignty over it from Poland in the Treaty of Wehlau in 1657 and built a standing army and a central tax administration across scattered domains that stretched from Cleves on the Rhine to <b>Pomerania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Swedish intervention rescued the Protestant cause in the Thirty Years’ War, and the Peace of Westphalia of 1648 gave Sweden <b>Western Pomerania</b>, Bremen-Verden and Wismar and a voice in the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In its first centuries Brandenburg was a conquering frontier power, extending its rule over the Slavs east of the Elbe and contending with <b>Pomerania</b>, Poland, the Archbishopric of Magdeburg and Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…and most of Livonia confirmed at the Truce of Altmark (1629), the mouth of the Oder River in <b>Pomerania</b> in the early 1630s, and the territories formalised in the Peace of Westphalia (1648), which gave Sweden Western Pomerania, the bishoprics of Bremen and Verden and the port of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
